  "id": "Compound::DB01050 - Gene::5243",
  "verbalization": "Ibuprofen and Abcb1 are linked through a mechanism involving the inhibition of cyclooxygenase (COX) and the binding of certain compounds to Abcb1. The evidence suggests that Ibuprofen, through its role in causing Reye's Syndrome or Wound Haemorrhage, indirectly influences Abcb1 via downstream molecules like Acetylsalicylic Acid or Dabigatran Etexilate. These molecules bind to Abcb1, indicating a potential regulatory role for Abcb1 in cellular processes affected by Ibuprofen. The shared lowest common ancestors—Carboxylic Acid and Carbonyl Compound—highlight a common biochemical pathway that connects Ibuprofen to Abcb1, suggesting a broader functional relationship. The evidence supports a dependency on Abcb1 in the cellular responses triggered by Ibuprofen, though the exact nature of this dependency remains to be fully elucidated.",
